7.3 The Consumption Function and Expenditure Schedule
Let consumption depend on autonomous consumption and disposable income: \[\begin{equation}
C=C_0+c(Y-T), \qquad 0&lt;c&lt;1,
\end{equation}\] where \(c\) is the marginal propensity to consume. In a simplified closed economy with fixed planned investment, government purchases, and lump-sum taxes, planned aggregate expenditure is: \[\begin{equation}
AE=C_0+c(Y-T)+I_p+G.
\end{equation}\] On a graph with income/output \(Y\) on the horizontal axis and planned expenditure \(AE\) on the vertical axis, draw a 45-degree line where \(AE=Y\) and the expenditure line with slope \(c\). Their intersection is short-run equilibrium. If the expenditure line shifts upward, equilibrium output rises by more than the initial autonomous-spending increase because each round of income induces additional consumption.

10.2 Fractional Reserve Banking and Multipliers
Banks expand deposits through commercial lending. Under fixed reserve requirements without excess reserve holding, the theoretical deposit multiplier is: \[\begin{equation}
m = \frac{1}{rr}
\end{equation}\]
